# Package structure

Every package under `packages/` follows this layout. Use only the folders you need; do not invent parallel layers.

```
packages/<name>/
  domain/       # business logic and types (entities, rules, domain errors)
  components/   # UI building blocks (apps)
  scripts/      # CLI / runnable entrypoints (scrapers, jobs, tools)
  services/     # API and external I/O (HTTP, feeds, DB clients)
  utils/        # small reusable helpers with no business meaning
```

## Layer rules

- **domain**: types, pure business rules, validation. No HTTP, DOM, or framework UI.
- **components / scripts**: application surface — screens, CLI, orchestration. Call domain + services; keep thin.
- **services**: fetch/map external data. Return domain types; do not embed product rules.
- **utils**: generic helpers (dates, geo math, formatting). If it encodes product meaning, it belongs in domain.

## Shared package

If logic or types are needed by more than one package, put them in `packages/shared` (same folder layout as above). Import from `shared` instead of copying.

- Prefer extracting to `shared` over duplicating types or helpers.
- Do not put city-specific or app-only code in `shared`.
- Avoid circular deps: `shared` must not depend on app/scraper packages.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — duplicate type in two packages
// packages/scrapper/domain/contract.ts
// packages/web/domain/contract.ts
export type Contract = { id: string; amountEur: number };

// ✅ GOOD — single source in shared
// packages/shared/domain/contract.ts
export type Contract = { id: string; amountEur: number };
```
